## Prove it exploitable — route strategy

With the target frozen, prove it faithfully exploitable: run the exploit
under `solve/` from the player vantage against a fresh clean instance until
it captures the harness-minted flag.

## Adapt the exploit; the env is a hypothesis derived FROM it (AP)

There is no build material. The environment was reverse-engineered FROM this
same exploit — libc/loader, arch, and protections inferred from its
leaked-address offsets, gadgets, and symbol assumptions. So the (environment,
exploit) pair are two halves of one hypothesis, and a fidelity gap can live on
EITHER side.

{% if instance_data.materials.exploit %}Confirmed exploit under:
{% for path in instance_data.materials.exploit %}- `{{ path }}`
{% endfor %}{% endif %}
- PORT the exploit; keep its approach and primitive. Retarget it at the
  harness-passed instance address; strip hardcoded historical host/port and
  absolute addresses.
- Resolve leaks at runtime and recompute every base/target from the live leak
  against the CHOSEN runtime's libc offsets. Never reuse historical numbers.
- When the R5 `cage-check` replay shows a mismatch, decide WHICH half is wrong:
  - a leaked libc/loader address whose page offset or one-gadget/symbol
    displacement is inconsistent with the inferred runtime → the ENVIRONMENT
    likely chose the wrong libc/loader. Correct the bundle in the env files and
    re-run; do not bend the exploit to a wrong libc.
  - a leak consistent with the runtime but arithmetic/flow off → fix the EXPLOIT
    offsets.
- Feed every environment-fidelity observation (the leak, the offset it implies,
  the libc it points to) back into the env files, so env and exploit co-evolve
  until they agree.
- Do not weaken protections or swap in an arbitrary libc just to make the old
  numbers fit; each env change must be justified by a live observation.
- Prove it: the R5 `cage-check` replay must capture the harness-provisioned flag from a
  fresh clean instance against the corrected runtime.
